=====================================================================
PROBE 6b  WHY k CAN NEVER REACH 3
=====================================================================
    m, n coprime cusp exponents with m, n >= 2  =>  m + n >= 5
    eps >= 1                                    =>  k >= 4
    minimum of k over m,n <= 40 coprime and eps <= 12 : 4
    attained only at (m,n) = (2,3), eps = 1 - Borisov's own cusp.

    ROBUSTNESS.  Dropping the step mu = m, the window would need
    G = mu(m+n)/m <= 4, i.e. mu <= 4m/(m+n) <= 4m/5 < m: y2's leading
    block would have to carry a g-power strictly below m, contradicting
    its being an m-th power at all.  The closure does not depend on it.

=====================================================================
FINAL STATE
=====================================================================
   D >= 4 : closed by the Belyi gate (probe 4c), uniformly in the cusp
            exponent, with no upper bound on D.
   D <= 3 : closed here - no cusp type can produce a contact exponent
            that low, and self-consistency forces D = k.

   Every gate is shut.  The published constructive framework family
   supports no Keller map, for any cusp type, cancellation depth,
   boundary order or chain degree.

   No counterexample was found.  This closes the constructive
   candidates; the plane Jacobian conjecture itself is untouched.
